 if you give  it the right intermittent forcing and so  if you run this  or enough data you can build a  reconstruction that looks very similar  to this embedded system except our blue  manifold is really coming from linear  Koopman dynamics with this special  intermittent forcing signature okay and  what's interesting is you actually see  that this intermittent forcing this  intermittent forcing signal if you look  at its probability distribution in the  very lower right it's non normal so the  the Gaussian is the white dashed line  it's very non normal it's got these long  tails corresponding to rare event  extreme forcing so these when these red  signals get really big that's rare and  it's non Gaussian and it happens to  correspond to when the lorentz system is  about to switch to the other lobe of the  attractor okay so whenever this red  signal gets big my system switches to  another lobe which is very cool so we're  gonna look at that again in a minutes  and this is similar to ideas of  intermittent forcing and extremer events  found in in work by saps esand mighta  and and others looking at things like  climate and ocean extreme ocean waves  and things like that so so there's some  really neat work out there looking at  intermittent forcing and we're taking  and looking at this intermittent forcing  for this Koopman linear system okay so  you can take all the pieces of that  havoc analysis on Lorentz you have a  system you measure some aspect of the  system you build time delay coordinates  and get an embedded attractor you can  then build a linear model with a forced  linear model you get a good and  reconstructed attractor when you do that  and you have some forcing statistics so  we have gone through and actually  applied this havoc analysis to lots of  systems from you know classical chaotic  system like Lorentz and Rossler to kind  of mechanical systems like the double  pendulum to more real-world relevant  systems like we have time series data  for a m